    {
      "$comment": "vulkan-headers, vulkan-loader and vulkan-memory-allocator are a matched SDK set and must move together: the loader's generated VulkanLoaderConfig.cmake hard-references the Vulkan::Headers target of its own release, so pinning one and leaving the others on a bare version>= lets a baseline move break the configure in whichever direction the unpinned side drifts (observed once already, headers pinned / loader floating -- see docs/spikes/M4-phase2a-test-harness.md \u00c2\u00a74b).",
      "name": "vulkan-headers",
      "version": "1.4.304.1#1"
    },
    {
      "name": "vulkan-loader",
      "version": "1.4.304.1"
    },
    {
      "name": "vulkan-memory-allocator",
      "version": "3.3.0"
    }
  ],
  "$comment-android": "Two !android gates remain -- amd-fidelityfx-cas and amd-fidelityfx-cacao -- and they are permanent rather than pending: upscaling is a non-goal (M12-if-ever, mobile-only, and the Vulkan backend keeps the stub), and both are Windows-shader-stack ports that would never belong in an Android closure. Every other gate lifted in M6 Task 12: the full carbon port set resolves and builds for arm64-android. What still keeps the engine out of the Android configuration is trinity/CMakeLists.txt's ANDROID early-return, not this manifest -- deliberately two separate facts now, and the early-return is the second half's to lift. One host constraint remains, and it is a fact about hosts rather than a gate: python3 cannot cross-build for android from a Windows host (CPython's pybuilddir.txt step runs sysconfig --generate-posix-vars, which a Windows build python cannot satisfy), and a POSIX-built artifact cannot be restored on Windows either, because vcpkg's ABI hash embeds host identity by design -- measured field by field in docs/spikes/artifacts/m6-python3-abi-probe-20260901.txt. The android port set is therefore built on a POSIX host."
}
